# Jobs, the places they are done in, and what I want to be with a reason.
lesson language-jobs v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=3 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "What do you want to be?"
  goal "Name jobs and where people do them in [[language.name]], and say what you want to be and why."

  look {
    scene 24x14 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        comic jobs who=["girl", "boy"] says=["[[phrase.what-do-you-want-to-be]]", "[[job.i-want-to-be-a-doctor]]"] moods=["happy", "happy"] with=["", ""] thinks=[0, 0] holds=["", ""] labels=["1", "2"]
      }
    }
    say """
      To ask about the future, say [[phrase.what-do-you-want-to-be]], what do you want to be? Leo
      answers [[job.i-want-to-be-a-doctor]], I want to be a doctor. Ana says
      [[job.i-want-to-be-a-teacher]] A doctor works in [[job.the-hospital]] and a cook in
      [[job.the-restaurant]]. Add a reason with [[opinion.because]].
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.jobs-name k=4
    }
    show language.jobs-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.jobs-meaning k=4 easy-k=1
    show language.jobs-write k=3 easy-k=2 hard-k=5
    show language.jobs-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.jobs-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.jobs-write k=4 easy-k=3 hard-k=2
    show language.jobs-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.jobs-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.jobs-write k=1
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show language.jobs-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.jobs-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[phrase.what-do-you-want-to-be]] What do you want to be? [[job.i-want-to-be-a-doctor]] I want to be a doctor."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is two jobs that care for someone taken for each other, the doctor
    and the vet, the teacher and the scientist: each naming question describes what the child wants
    to do and offers the job most often mixed up. In [[language.name]] many jobs change their ending
    for a woman (médico, médica; profesor, profesora), which is why Ana and Leo say them
    differently; a language without this says them the same. The step up from where you want to go,
    in grade five, is I want with a job and a reason, and the place it is done in. From this grade
    the written words are marked with their accents: a word typed without its accent is not taken.
    The dictation is read from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud, or played by the device.
    If the child is stuck, name the jobs of people the child knows, one at a time. Off the page,
    interview a grown-up about their job in the language: where, what and why. It meets England's
    key stage 2 languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "speak in sentences, using
    familiar vocabulary, phrases and basic language structures"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign
    languages: "Can talk about their own thoughts and feelings regarding familiar and simple matters
    after organizing the contents they want to communicate" (Japan's grade six book We Can! 2 has a
    unit on what you want to be, recalled); and CEFR A2, "Can understand sentences and frequently
    used expressions related to areas of most immediate relevance (e.g. very basic personal and
    family information, shopping, local geography, employment)". The phrases are in
    [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[job.i-want-to-be-a-pilot]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[job.i-want-to-be-a-doctor]] and [[job.i-want-to-be-a-teacher]] and the words to write are [[job.cook]] and [[job.astronaut]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[job.i-want-to-be-an-astronaut]] and [[job.i-want-to-be-a-footballer]] and the words to write are [[job.scientist]], [[job.cook]] and [[job.pilot]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
